- How would you adapt if wordDict contains duplicates or extremely long words?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design both memoized and tabulated DP for a non-trivial partition constraint, reinforcing DP patterns and edge-case handling.

## Key Concepts
- Top-down memoization with arrays
- Bottom-up tabulation
- Prefix sums for O(1) range sums
- Power-of-two check with bit trick

## Code Example
```javascript
function countPartitions(nums){
  const n = nums.length;
  const pref = new Array(n+1).fill(0);
  for(let i=0;i<n;i++) pref[i+1]=pref[i]+nums[i];
  const isPow2 = (x)=> x>0 && (x&(x-1))===0;
  // Memoized top-down
  const memo = new Array(n+1).fill(-1);
  const top = (i)=>{
    if(i===n) return 1;
    if(memo[i]!==-1) return memo[i];
    let res = 0;
    for(let j=i+1;j<=n;j++){
      const s = pref[j]-pref[i];
      if(isPow2(s)) res += top(j);
    }
    memo[i]=res; return res;
  };
  // Bottom-up
  const dp = new Array(n+1).fill(0);
  dp[n]=1;
  for(let i=n-1;i>=0;i--){
    let sumWays=0;
    for(let j=i+1;j<=n;j++){
      const s = pref[j]-pref[i];
      if(isPow2(s)) sumWays += dp[j];
    }
    dp[i]=sumWays;
  }
  return {topWays: top(0), bottomWays: dp[0]};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you optimize for large n or multiple queries on the same array?
- How would you handle large counts and modulo arithmetic to prevent overflow?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests DP with dual objectives: minimize cost and count optimal deletion sets, plus implement in two styles. It also covers edge cases like empty substrings and ties.

## Key Concepts
- DP on substrings; store (cost, ways).
- Memoization and bottom-up parallels; careful counting on ties.
- Modulo arithmetic for counts.

## Code Example
```javascript
// Memoized DFS
function solveMemo(s, delCost){
  const n = s.length;
  const memoCost = Array.from({length:n},()=>Array(n).fill(-1));
  const memoWays = Array.from({length:n},()=>Array(n).fill(0));
  const MOD = 1_000_000_007;
  function dfs(l,r){
    if (l>=r) return {cost:0, ways:1};
    if (memoCost[l][r]!==-1) return {cost:memoCost[l][r], ways:memoWays[l][r]};
    if (s[l]===s[r]){
      const res = dfs(l+1,r-1);
      memoCost[l][r]=res.cost;
      memoWays[l][r]=res.ways;
      return res;
    } else {
      const A = dfs(l+1,r);
      const B = dfs(l,r-1);
      let cost;
      let ways = 0;
      if (A.cost + delCost[l] < B.cost + delCost[r]){
        cost = A.cost + delCost[l];
        ways = A.ways;
      } else if (B.cost + delCost[r] < A.cost + delCost[l]){
        cost = B.cost + delCost[r];
        ways = B.ways;
      } else {
        cost = A.cost + Math.min(delCost[l], delCost[r]);
        ways = (A.ways + B.ways) % MOD;
      }
      memoCost[l][r]=cost; memoWays[l][r]=ways;
      return {cost, ways};
    }
  }
  return dfs(0,n-1);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This probes understanding of iterative approaches, partition logic, and complexity trade-offs between quicksort and mergesort. 

## Key Concepts
- Iterative quicksort with explicit stack
- Hoare partition scheme and pivot strategy
- Time/space complexity comparisons with mergesort

## Code Example
```javascript
function iterativeQuicksort(a){
  const n=a.length;
  const stack=[[0,n-1]];
  const swap=(i,j)=>{let t=a[i]; a[i]=a[j]; a[j]=t;};
  while(stack.length){
    const [l,r]=stack.pop();
    if(l>=r) continue;
    let pivot=a[(l+r)>>1];
    let i=l, j=r;
    while(i<=j){
      while(a[i]<pivot) i++;
      while(a[j]>pivot) j--;
      if(i<=j){ swap(i,j); i++; j--; }
    }
    if(l<j) stack.push([l,j]);
    if(i<r) stack.push([i,r]);
  }
  return a;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you modify to guarantee O(n log n) worst-case?
- Compare stability with mergesort; when would you choose one over the other?

